-- src/eval/journal/scan.ail
--
-- ADR-004 v5 D6 "Scan policy", "An entry", "Location and handling";
-- PLAN-004 §3 P1.8: the scan policy, the entry and the program artifact.
--
-- THE POLICY (reviewed and versioned with E; `scan_policy_version()`). Every
-- string of every component is scanned:
--
--   snapshot            one site per line (`Source(Snapshot, line)`, 0-based)
--   excerpt             one site per line (`Source(Excerpt, line)`)
--   program identity    each identity component (`Interaction(ordinal)`)
--   program projection  the request projection
--   program payload     the outcome payload
--   program chunks      each stream chunk (field `chunk:<n>`)
--   environment         each entry's key and value (`Aggregate("env:<i>")`);
--                       the key also through `name_axis`
--   files               each file's path and content (`Aggregate("file:<i>")`)
--   metadata            the program's generator, initial-world and manifest
--                       strings (`Aggregate("program")`) and the entry's own
--                       records (`Aggregate(<file name>)`)
--   derived output      every file a run writes under `runs/`
--
-- and each site yields:
--
--   REFUSE  `PrivateKeyBlock`, `JsonWebToken`, `UrlUserinfo` (dst_secrets's
--           shape rules, through `scan_text`, unchanged), and
--           `ProviderTokenLiteral` when ANY occurrence of ANY of the 23
--           prefixes is followed by >= `body_threshold()` (16) characters of
--           the credential alphabet. Every occurrence is enumerated here;
--           `scan_text` reports only the first prefix that matches and says
--           nothing about what follows it, so its `ProviderTokenLiteral` is
--           dropped and replaced by this enumeration.
--   REPORT  everything else, by site, reason, count and body length:
--           `OpaqueHighEntropy` (body length = the run), a prefix whose
--           longest body is < 16, and `CredentialBearingName` on an
--           environment key (and an environment read's key), which is
--           REPORT-ONLY by this policy's explicit rule: `encode_artifact`'s
--           name-axis refusal is not inherited, and the finding is not
--           dropped.
--
-- A report row carries component, location, field, reason, count, body
-- length and disposition; NEVER the body. The prefix and the name token are
-- the published list entries, not content. For the shape rules the count is
-- 1 (presence: `scan_text` reports each shape once per string).
--
-- BODY-16 IS A HEURISTIC (v5 D6), and its limits are stated, not fixed:
--   false negatives  a short body can still be a credential: invented short
--                    `Basic `/`Bearer `/`sk-`/`npm_` probes match the prefix
--                    and pass body-16 (report only);
--   false positives  a long body occurs in quoted examples and ordinary prose
--                    (`sk-` inside a hyphenated word followed by 16 letters,
--                    `ASIA` inside a longer upper-case word);
--   and the prefixes are matched anywhere, not at a word boundary. The
--   threshold lives in this copy, not in `dst_secrets` (which is unchanged);
--   changing it is a policy change and re-admits the corpus.
--
-- THE ARTIFACT. `"<schema>\ndigest\t<digest>\n<body>\n"` from `encode_body`
-- and `program_digest` (dst_persistence.ail:544, :591), loaded by
-- `load_program` (:1344), which verifies the digest (:1113). No second codec:
-- the bytes are `encode_artifact`'s for a program its gate admits; this
-- policy, not that gate (which refuses an ordinary long repository path), is
-- what decides whether the program is written.
-- WHY `program_artifact` DOES NOT CALL `encode_artifact` (P1R C7):
-- `encode_artifact` (dst_persistence.ail:663) is `scan_program` then the same
-- frame; its gate refuses what D6's policy explicitly reports and admits — a
-- credential-bearing environment NAME (report-only here) and the opaque-run
-- heuristic on paths. Calling it would re-impose the gate D6 replaced (and
-- `EncodeRefused` would leave no bytes to write). The frame is the only
-- thing re-spelled, and `scan_live_test.ail` asserts both halves: equal
-- bytes wherever the gate encodes, and a credential-bearing name that the
-- gate refuses while this policy reports it.
--
-- THE ENTRY. `.motoko/eval-corpus/<entry>/`, directories 0700, files 0600:
--   snapshot.jsonl, excerpt.jsonl, sources.sha256 (`sha256sum -c` format),
--   selector.txt, expectations.json, t0_settings.json, expected_end.txt,
--   program.artifact, witness.json, census.txt (family and census record),
--   envelope.json, identities.json, scan_report.tsv, exposure.jsonl
--   (pre-entry rows migrated first, each wrapped as
--   `{"migrated_from": <log>, "row": <row>}`, then the entry's own rows),
--   and an empty `runs/` for everything derived.
-- `write_entry` scans first (a refusal writes nothing), then builds the whole
-- entry in `<root>/.staging-<entry>` (install -d -m 0700; each file written
-- then chmod 0600 inside the 0700 directory, so no file is readable by
-- another user between the two), checks every mode with `stat`, and renames
-- the staging directory onto `<root>/<entry>` (`mv -n -T`). Any failure
-- removes the staging directory and is a REFUSAL; an existing entry or
-- staging directory is never overwritten. So a permission failure is a
-- refusal, not a partial entry.
--
-- WHAT IT DOES NOT DO. It renders none of the records it is given (witness,
-- census, envelope, identities, selector, expectations, T0 settings, end):
-- those are the runner's (P1.6/P1.7b/P1.9a), passed in as text and scanned as
-- metadata here. `witness.json` and `census.txt` are the entry-comparand
-- records K1–K7 read, and their one codec is `candidate_checks_run.
-- entry_json` / `decode_entry` (P1R C6); the runner's admit mode writes them
-- (EVAL_ENTRY_OUT), and an `EntryInputs` built for a real entry takes them
-- from `entry_json` — the fixture texts below are scan-policy test data, not
-- entry records. It does not scan the exposure log (agent records this code
-- writes, not corpus content).
--
-- Privacy (§0.6): synthetic fixtures only; reports carry counts, lengths,
-- sites and identities.
--
-- THE PINS. The copies below carry `SOURCE` pins at A
-- (65003110ff5a15e2ae5b1f0e205e0ffa705d18dc), as in `digests.ail`; bodies are
-- the source bodies with names prefixed `sc_`.

